Are birds the only egg laying animals that raise their young for a significant period after they hatch?
Not at all. Birds are probably the most conspicuous egg-layers that provide prolonged parental care, but there are some fascinating non-bird examples—and a few get surprisingly close to what we'd call "raising" offspring.
• Crocodilians — perhaps the clearest non-bird example. Mothers guard nests, help hatchlings out, carry them in their mouths to water, protect nursery groups, and respond to distress calls. Young can remain near and receive protection from the mother for months and sometimes over a year. Given that crocodilians and birds are both archosaurs, this strongly suggests substantial parental care has very deep evolutionary roots in that lineage.
• Some reptiles besides crocodilians do considerably more parenting than the stereotypical "lay eggs and leave." Certain skinks, for example, remain with offspring and defend family groups. Some lizards show prolonged parent-offspring associations, although active provisioning is uncommon.
• Poison frogs and other amphibians can be extraordinary parents. Depending on the species, adults guard eggs, transport tadpoles on their backs, deposit them in individual pools, and sometimes return repeatedly to feed them. Female Oophaga poison frogs lay unfertilized "trophic" eggs specifically for their tadpoles to eat. That's genuine post-hatching provisioning.
• Fish contain a huge range of parental strategies. Cichlids are especially impressive: parents guard eggs and fry, herd their offspring around, defend territories, and sometimes allow young to feed on mucus secreted from their skin. Discus fish are a famous example. Some cichlid offspring remain dependent on parental protection for weeks or months.
• Invertebrates provide some wonderfully weird cases. Earwig mothers guard eggs, clean them, defend newly hatched nymphs and sometimes feed them. Burying beetles prepare a vertebrate carcass as a nursery and then regurgitate food into their larvae's mouths, remarkably reminiscent of birds. Some spiders and other arthropods protect young for extended periods, and a few extreme cases involve offspring eventually eating their mother.
So if by "raise" you mean continued, active investment after hatching rather than merely guarding eggs, birds aren't unique at all.
What is remarkable about birds is the combination of egg-laying + near-universal parental care + active feeding + relatively prolonged juvenile dependence. That package is unusually pervasive across an entire major animal lineage.
And there's a neat evolutionary connection hiding here: birds' closest living relatives are crocodilians, which happen to be among the most attentive reptilian parents. That makes the bird/crocodilian similarity considerably more interesting than coincidence.
Are there genetically closer related species to humans that those two groups that don't share this common set of traits?
Yes—very much so. And I think you're noticing an interesting phylogenetic pattern.
If by "those two groups" you mean birds and crocodilians, there are many groups more closely related to humans than either is, yet they don't share that particular package of egg-laying plus prolonged post-hatching parental care.
The evolutionary tree is roughly:
vertebrates → tetrapods → amniotes →
• Synapsids → mammals → humans
• Sauropsids → reptiles
◦ turtles
◦ lepidosaurs (lizards, snakes, tuatara)
◦ archosaurs → crocodilians + birds
So humans are on the synapsid side of the great amniote split, while birds and crocodilians are on the sauropsid side.
There are actually some excellent counterexamples between that split and modern humans. Monotremes—platypuses and echidnas—are much more closely related to us than birds or crocodilians are. They lay eggs and provide substantial post-hatching care, including milk. But most other mammals abandoned egg-laying entirely.
Meanwhile, many sauropsids that are evolutionarily positioned between the common amniote ancestor and the bird/crocodilian archosaur lineage don't exhibit the same characteristic combination. Most turtles, snakes, and lizards lay eggs but generally provide little or no prolonged post-hatching care, although there are important exceptions.
And that produces an interesting pattern:
Crocodilians: eggs + extensive parental care
Birds: eggs + extensive parental care
Most turtles/lizards/snakes: eggs + little/no post-hatching care
Monotremes: eggs + extensive parental care
Marsupial/placental mammals: live birth + extensive parental care
That matters because evolutionary relatedness doesn't imply that every intervening lineage should retain a trait. Traits can be lost, modified, independently evolved, or inherited from a much older ancestor and subsequently lost by other descendants.
In fact, birds and crocodilians sharing elaborate parental behavior is particularly informative because they're each other's closest living relatives: together they constitute the living Archosauria. Evidence from dinosaurs—including nesting colonies, brooding postures, and associations of adults with juveniles—also makes some form of substantial parental behavior in extinct archosaurs quite plausible.
So if the question behind your question is "Could prolonged parental care be an ancestral characteristic that some descendant groups subsequently lost, rather than something birds independently invented?"—absolutely. That's actually one of the important hypotheses researchers can test by mapping parental behaviors onto the evolutionary tree.
And there's an even bigger version of your observation: parental care has appeared, disappeared, and changed form repeatedly across vertebrate evolution. That makes it unusually useful for asking what ecological or structural conditions make raising offspring evolutionarily worthwhile.
