Yes. And I think the distinction you're making is more interesting than simply "does the species provide parental care?" You're pointing toward how unfinished the offspring is when it enters the outside world, and how much development consequently occurs through interaction with parents and environment.
Humans are extreme on that dimension. Human newborns are neurologically and physically immature, and human juveniles remain dependent for an extraordinary fraction of the lifespan. There's a good evolutionary reason for some of this—our developmental strategy involves enormous postnatal brain growth—but the older simple explanation that humans are born early primarily because our heads otherwise couldn't fit through the pelvis ("the obstetrical dilemma") is now regarded as incomplete. Maternal metabolic constraints, pelvic mechanics, brain development, and other factors probably all contribute.
Birds produce an unexpectedly interesting parallel. Bird development ranges from precocial to altricial:
A duckling can hatch feathered, mobile, visually functional, and capable of feeding itself fairly quickly. A songbird can hatch blind, nearly naked, unable to thermoregulate adequately, unable to obtain food, and essentially helpless. Its parents then finish an enormous amount of its development externally.
Crocodilians aren't nearly as developmentally helpless when they hatch—they can move, swim and feed—but nevertheless depend heavily upon adults for protection and access to suitable nursery habitat. So they're less analogous to humans in developmental immaturity than many birds are.
And your mammal comparison is particularly revealing. Consider a wildebeest, horse, deer, or antelope. The newborn has a mother and absolutely receives parental investment, but it's dramatically more operational at birth. Within hours it can stand, walk, follow its mother, perceive its surroundings effectively, and participate in escape behavior.
A newborn human is hilariously useless by comparison.
Yet that doesn't straightforwardly make the wildebeest developmentally "more advanced." It represents a different allocation of development:
precocial strategy: do more construction before birth/hatching → offspring arrives relatively operational.
altricial strategy: move more construction after birth/hatching → offspring arrives unfinished and develops while embedded in a parental/social environment.
And that's where your observation becomes quite interesting.
Extreme altriciality can potentially do something that prenatal development cannot: allow the environment to participate in constructing the mature phenotype.
For humans, an enormous amount of neural development occurs while the infant is already receiving sensory information, interacting socially, acquiring motor control, hearing language, forming attachments, and eventually learning cultural information. The brain isn't simply getting larger after birth; it's developing while exposed to the world it must model.
Many birds offer a strikingly convergent version of this. Particularly in parrots, corvids and some songbirds, prolonged juvenile development accompanies substantial social learning, vocal learning, play, behavioral flexibility, and learning from adults. Parrots and corvids also happen to be among the animals whose cognitive abilities most routinely surprise us despite their enormous evolutionary distance from mammals.
So I would slightly reformulate the pattern you've noticed:
Perhaps the interesting variable isn't parental care itself, but the extent to which a lineage has shifted development from a protected internal environment into a protected interactive environment.
That would separate a horse from a human despite both being intensely cared-for mammals, and separate a chicken from some highly altricial songbirds despite both being cared-for birds.
It also avoids the anthropocentric "advanced animal" hierarchy you mentioned. Evolutionarily, a horse isn't more advanced than a crow, nor is a human more evolved than either. But organisms can differ enormously in when they commit developmental resources and how much information from the external/social environment becomes available during development.
There is even a lovely conceptual inversion here: helplessness can itself be an adaptation.
Being born highly competent locks in more of the organism before environmental information becomes available. Being born unfinished is initially enormously expensive and dangerous—but if parents can keep the offspring alive, that extended developmental window may buy something extremely valuable:
plasticity.
And humans may represent an extraordinarily extreme implementation of that tradeoff.
