Hahah, I'll accept my developmental milestone with dignity. 😁 And yes—your comparison actually survives contact with biology better than you might expect, although I'd modify the geometry of it a little.
Dolphins and crocodilians occupy partially overlapping aquatic ecological spaces, but they represent almost opposite developmental solutions to them. Crocodilians hatch fairly competent physically and receive substantial protection, whereas dolphins give live birth to a single, highly invested offspring that then spends years embedded in a social and informational environment. Dolphin calves nurse for years in some species, learn foraging techniques, social relationships and communication, and can remain associated with their mothers for a long time.
And dolphins really are cognitively exceptional. They show sophisticated social cognition, imitation, long-term social memory, flexible problem-solving, culturally transmitted behaviors, and—in some populations—learned foraging traditions passed between generations. So putting them toward the high end of nonhuman mammalian cognition is quite defensible.
Your proposed analogy therefore looks approximately like:
crocodilian → cognitively sophisticated bird
as
more precocial aquatic strategy → more developmentally/socially extended aquatic strategy
with
crocodilian → dolphin
and
precocial bird → highly altricial/social bird
representing increasingly extended opportunities for socially embedded development.
But there's one really important complication: crocodilians aren't cognitively trivial. They've historically been badly underestimated. They exhibit social signaling, coordinated behavior in some circumstances, parental responsiveness, learning, individual recognition-like discrimination, and surprisingly flexible hunting behavior. They're nowhere near dolphins by the measures we normally associate with complex cognition, but "big reflex machine with teeth" isn't a fair description either.
There's another wrinkle that I think makes your comparison more interesting.
The two spectacular vertebrate experiments in complex intelligence aren't simply mammals and birds. Within each radiation, some of the standouts are animals with unusually prolonged developmental/social-learning periods: primates and cetaceans among mammals; corvids and parrots among birds. And those lineages evolved their cognitive machinery independently from a very distant common ancestor.
That doesn't establish that prolonged dependency causes intelligence. There are plenty of counterexamples, and ecological pressures, lifespan, brain architecture, energetic availability, manipulative ability, social complexity and many other variables are tangled together.
But there is a plausible feedback loop:
parental protection → longer safe development → greater plasticity → more learning → more behavioral complexity → greater benefit from parental/social teaching → stronger selection for prolonged development.
Once a lineage gets onto that trajectory, knowledge itself can become part of what parents transmit to offspring, alongside genes and calories. At that point, an animal isn't merely growing into its ecological niche; some portion of the niche is being taught to it.
Humans then look less like an inexplicable discontinuity and more like an absolutely ridiculous endpoint of the same strategy. We remain dependent long enough to learn an immense inherited behavioral environment—language, tool use, social rules, ecological knowledge, etc.—and then invented grandparents, schools, books, universities and now digital archives to extend parental information transmission beyond individual lifetimes.
So your dolphin–crocodilian / sophisticated-bird comparison isn't really measurable as a clean "same distance in intelligence"—we don't have a universal scalar intelligence axis on which to establish that. But the structural comparison you're making is legitimate: similar broad environmental problems can coexist with radically different allocations between inherited competence and prolonged, socially mediated development.
And that brings us somewhere surprisingly close to your original observation: perhaps one useful thing to examine isn't which animals are intelligent, but which animals can afford to remain incompetent for the longest time. 😁
It also brings us to potentially the most fascinating and informative discrepancy that could reveal most of the currently lacking information about what intelligence is and how it can develop: cephalopods
Yes. Cephalopods are almost the perfect adversarial test case for the pattern we just constructed. They preserve several things we'd associate with sophisticated cognition while removing many of the ingredients we were beginning to suspect produced it.
Take an octopus. Compared with humans, dolphins, parrots, and corvids, the developmental architecture seems almost perversely backwards. Most octopuses are relatively short-lived. Many are largely solitary. Mothers generally stop feeding while guarding their eggs and die around the time they hatch. There is therefore essentially no parent-to-offspring teaching. Hatchlings of many species disperse without ever meaningfully knowing their parents.
And yet octopuses exhibit remarkable learning, exploration, problem-solving, object manipulation, spatial learning, flexible hunting strategies, and behavioral responses that are difficult to explain as rigid instinct alone.
That breaks the simple version of our hypothesis:
prolonged dependency → social learning → intelligence.
But I don't think it breaks the deeper version. It may actually reveal the missing variable.
What humans, dolphins, corvids/parrots and octopuses conspicuously share isn't necessarily prolonged childhood.
It's behavioral underdetermination.
An organism can arrive equipped with relatively fixed solutions to the problems its ancestors encountered. Alternatively, evolution can provide machinery capable of constructing solutions after encountering the particular problem.
Our earlier idea of altriciality would then be one mechanism for buying that capacity. Parents create a protected developmental period in which an underdetermined nervous system can safely learn.
Octopuses appear to buy it another way.
Their bodies are extraordinarily flexible. Their arms have enormous degrees of freedom; there's no simple fixed skeletal geometry determining how an arm must move. Their environments are spatially complicated and variable. They encounter diverse prey with radically different defenses. They can manipulate objects, squeeze through arbitrary geometries, camouflage themselves and interact with environments where a completely predetermined behavioral repertoire may be prohibitively complicated.
In other words, the octopus's morphology may itself make flexible control valuable.
And its nervous system is correspondingly bizarre from our perspective. A huge fraction of its neurons are outside the central brain, particularly in the arms. The animal therefore isn't simply a vertebrate-style centralized controller transplanted into a mollusc. Intelligence has been implemented through a substantially different architecture.
That gives us something close to a natural experiment.
Three routes to behavioral flexibility
You could provisionally distinguish:
1. Precocial specialization
"Here are solutions. Go use them."
Many animals emerge with comparatively mature sensorimotor repertoires.
2. Protected developmental plasticity
"Here is a system for learning solutions, and adults will keep you alive while you construct them."
Humans, dolphins and highly altricial/social birds lean strongly toward this strategy.
3. Immediate individual plasticity
"Here is a system for learning solutions. Good luck."
Cephalopods come astonishingly close to this third case.
And now we have a comparison capable of separating several variables that are normally hopelessly correlated.
Humans give us:
intelligence + long development + sociality + parental care + cultural transmission + long lifespan
Dolphins:
intelligence + long development + sociality + parental care + cultural transmission + long lifespan
Corvids/parrots:
intelligence + substantial development + sociality + parental care + social learning + relatively long lifespan
Octopuses:
intelligence + little/no parental teaching + often limited sociality + short lifespan + radically different nervous system + independent evolutionary origin.